# New Professors!

Man is but one among the countless living creatures. Yet he thinks himself superior to every other creature. The saying, “Rare, rare indeed, is birth as a human being,” has strengthened that belief. Man mocks other creatures by using them as comparisons: he toils like an ox, wanders like a dog, hoots like an owl, and crawls like a tortoise. He speaks of a monkey’s mischief, a fox’s cunning, a tiger’s nature, and a snake’s fangs. Sometimes, when caught in infatuation, he says that she cooed like a cuckoo and danced like a peacock. For the most part, however, man imagines that he has attained a position above all other living beings. He uses expressions such as “beastliness” and “the condition of a beast” to denote degradation.

Who will act as counsel for the animals? Though we enjoy the cuckoo’s song, who will accept it as a fee and bring a defamation suit against human society? Even if someone did, the other creatures would object if the judge were human. Man, for his part, would never concede that another creature had the right to try him. God might be summoned; but if He were, resourceful man would arrive with half a measure of camphor. Who can say how the wheels of justice would then turn?

A few people have argued on behalf of other living creatures. I do not mean members of societies for the prevention of cruelty to animals. Some thinkers and poets have pointed out that animals and birds, which man disparages, possess rare qualities absent in mankind. The chambers built by the mole and the beaver cannot be built by man. How wondrous are the cells made by honeybees!

Is man alone acquainted with the use of weapons? Monkeys throw sticks and leaves at their enemies. Man has merely made greater use of the objects that come within reach of his hands; there is no other special distinction.

“If education is said to make a difference, mother monkeys teach their young: do not do this; do that. Birds demonstrate flight to their chicks. Cats teach their kittens to catch mice. Man, who evolved from animals, obtained at least a part of the knowledge required for his life from the lives of animals themselves,” said Comrade M. Singaravelar. His argument did not stop there.

“Man is more cruel than animals, for he has created evils in life. Those evils are three:

1. He converted the three things required for common use, food, clothing, and shelter, into private property.

2. He imposed the system called profit upon articles meant for use, extended it through such exactions as land tax and poll tax, and thereby denied the majority an adequate supply of life’s necessities.

3. Instead of allowing the things needed for human life to be distributed in common, he allowed only a few to control the means by which they are produced.

“As a consequence of these three evils created by man, there arose famine, starvation, disease, robbery, murder, war, and similar calamities, as well as deceit, falsehood, fraud, and gambling. Why, then, should man be called some great genius superior to animals?” the venerable elder asked. These were not merely words of wisdom capable of engaging one’s thought for hours and days; they were words that made conceited man hang his head in shame.

Similarly, Kavimani Desika Vinayagam, distressed by the evils and cruelties in the people’s way of life, wished to impart wisdom to human society. The ordinary method would be to cite the words of the Buddha or of ancient sages. But Kavimani was seventy years old. He had spent his life composing poem after poem, yet the cruelties in the people’s lives had not disappeared. Perhaps he thought, “Enough! These humans need new professors,” and summoned animals to instruct mankind and mock its arrogance.

> Have the wolf and fox, the leopard and tiger of the forest  
> Begun to laugh on seeing the nation’s civilization?

Thus he sang. He says that the people of the country live in a manner fit to make animals laugh in ridicule. Man mocked the animals; the poet asks the animals to mock man.

Some days ago, a comrade writing under the name “Surabhi,” in the Tamil journal *Sakthi*, argued on behalf of a creature reviled by man, much as the late M. Singaravelar had done. I think the gentleman was rather courageous, for he argued on behalf of the snake!

Yes, indeed, the snake with venomous fangs, the snake we strike the moment we see it. In simple and pleasing verse, and with reasons difficult to refute, he argues its case by making the snake itself speak. The language is sweet Tamil. The poem’s idea is this.

A man sees a snake and a doubt arises in him. Why should so cruel a snake have been created? Even if God had to create it, why did He not give it a repulsive form? Why give it a gleaming body? God did not appear before him, so he could not ask Him. The man decided to ask the snake instead. Venom within, a captivating garment without! “O snake, why this magnificent life for one who does such cruelty?” he dared to ask:

> Snake, who was it that created you?  
> Who gave you that gleaming body?  
> Though one may feed you sweet milk and nectar,  
> Who gave you a mind that hisses?

Even when fed and reared with milk, a snake will bite; and when it bites, its venom will spread. The man asks the snake this same question through several verses. The snake replies.

Would the snake not be angry that man had asked so ordinary a question? It therefore answers with disdain:

> The noble One who created you,  
> The mighty One who ordered the world,  
> Created me too within this world  
> And endowed me with a lovely form.

The snake knows that the man’s question arose not from ignorance but arrogance. It therefore resolves to give him a scorching answer:

> Unlike man, who claims that inside and out  
> Are one, and that he is the lord of the world,  
> We snakes are not, indeed,  
> The crueler ones.

The snake declares that snakes are no more cruel than the humans who boast that none on earth equals them. It has begun to speak for its whole species. Imagine the man’s astonishment. What is he to think when a snake looks at him and says, “I am no crueler than you”?

“What audacity! What a brazen lie from a snake that goes about with poison in its fangs!” the man might think. The snake understands this. Had it merely wished to trade insults, it could have said, “I am no worse than you,” and slithered away. But the snake is not merely abusing him; it is making an accusation. It therefore boldly presents its case and supplies reasons that leave man struggling for an answer:

> We do not cover our bare bodies in silk;  
> We do not deck ourselves in costly ornaments.  
> We do not discourse on laws and scriptures;  
> We do not make cunning itself our sacred spell.  
> We do not strike the poor in their empty bellies;  
> We do not parade as supreme benefactors.  
> We do not chant five-lettered and six-lettered formulas  
> While nurturing sin within our hearts.  
> We do not wrap ourselves in khadi white as thumba flowers;  
> We do not roam about as followers and devotees.  
> We do not shout “Govinda!” in a crowd;  
> We do not shamelessly trade on the black market.

Having pounded man breathless with so many blows, the snake mocks him too:

> Bearing the body the Holy One gave us,  
> We dwell within an anthill and live, sir.  
> The moment we see the creature called man,  
> We flee a mile away and hide!

The snake leaves after establishing, with evidence, that man is a cheat and a hypocrite. What answer can man give? What answer is there to give?

The difference between the former use poets made of the snake and its use here is not merely noteworthy; it deserves close attention. This is part of a revolution in art, evidence that the method is changing. This innovative poetic spirit flowers and bears abundant fruit in many places in the work of the revolutionary poet Bharathidasan.

Consider one example: pigeons. What his eyes see is not merely pigeons:

> Like the petals of a lotus spread open,  
> The pigeons gather in a circle.

Does he see beauty alone? He sees a precious lesson too, and conveys it to us:

> In their life there is no stabbing or cutting;  
> Though each is different, none is barred from eating;  
> There is no blind custom of high and low.

Man ought surely to hang his head. Is there not segregation even at meals among university students who study world history? “Look at these pigeons, man! Though they are different, there is no restriction on eating together and no blind custom of lower and upper,” the revolutionary poet says, striking home.

Many watch pigeons, but what fills them with delight is the birds’ courtship. The poet knows this. From that kingdom of love, he draws attention to its justice so that humans may learn.

Even when he has a woman of golden beauty as his own, a man may lose his heart upon seeing another woman made a little glittering by the dressmaker’s or beautician’s skill. To that male world, the poet points to the pigeon and offers instruction:

> A hen pigeon, they say, will not mate  
> With any but her own cock;  
> Though another hen should stand there  
> Flashing like fireworks, another cock will not even turn to look!

He says this for the bulls whose eyes and thoughts wander wherever they go.

Some may ask, “Does it always happen so? Does no improper act ever occur?” Knowing this, the poet says:

> She was alone and did not consent;  
> He wronged her, causing her distress.

Thus he explains the circumstances in which the “difficulty” arises. The hen pigeon is alone, her mate absent. Another pigeon forces himself upon her and wrongs her, causing distress. Even Ahalya, wife of a sage who had renounced everything, is said in sacred legend to have met such a fate! Is it then monstrous that wrongdoing should occur among pigeons? Have they studied the Agamas or practised yoga? Of Ahalya, one poet says she recognized the danger, was bewildered, and resisted; another says she recognized it and rejoiced proudly. When the pigeon does wrong, our poet says only that he wrongs her and causes her distress.

“Whether it brings distress or pleasure, is not a wrong still a wrong?” we may ask. The poet leaves no room even for that objection. Nor does he waste the occasion to teach man wisdom. The improper act has happened. Why?

> Among pigeons in whom love abides,  
> A few wastrels may perhaps  
> Have learned such conduct  
> From sorrow-laden humankind.

Does one need still more heat? Is that not enough?

The poets of old employed birds to carry messages, sing and dance in groves, or live in the cages of beautiful women. They never gave birds the office of professor as here. The snake was used as a couch for gods or as their ornament, but was never invited to teach mankind. Ancient poets sang only:

> O cobra resting upon the Lord’s crown,  
> O good snake that carries a pouch of venom,  
> O hooded snake dwelling beneath the earth,  
> Stand, sing, and dance, O snake!

They did not sing of the snake coming to teach a lesson.

We welcome this not merely as new verse but as a new method, for we desire a path by which clarity of thought may be introduced through art. Bharathidasan is the architect of this artistic revolution. It is not merely that he composed such poems; he awakened in others the desire to compose them. He did not merely write poetry; he created that kind of poetic consciousness.

Among those who move within the poet’s poetic sphere is Comrade Puduvai Sivaprakasam. Eight years ago he composed a poem in this new manner.

In Surabhi’s poem published in the June 1946 issue of *Sakthi*, a snake mocks man. In Puduvai Sivaprakasam’s poem, an entire company of birds joins together and puts him through an ordeal. He has done no wrong. Hoping to write a poem and enjoy the pleasure it brings, he goes to a grove. Parrots, mynahs, crows, a royal eagle, and many other birds are there. Would the winged creatures remain quiet? The moment they see him, they begin attacking humankind, and Tamils in particular. They condemn and ridicule caste divisions among Tamils, their mystical doctrines, enslavement, and superstitious customs.

Hearing them, the poet grows angry. “How dare these birds ridicule my Tamil people so?” he thinks. He longs to answer them and silence their tongues, but cannot. Why? Everything the birds say is an irrefutable truth.

> Shall these wretched birds make mockery of me?  
> Shall I depart in silence without curbing their tongues?  
> Thinking thus, I came forward, seeking a reply to give;  
> But I could not do it!

So says the poet. It appears that neither can we answer the birds’ satire. Here is his poem; see for yourselves:

> Seeking a broad grove where cool streams flow,  
> I went there to compose a well-tuned poem.  
> Upon a tree, birds of rare beauty  
> Had gathered, exchanging greetings in delight.  
> They made a mighty joyful sound; but at my arrival  
> They started and stopped their speech. For a few moments  
> There was no murmur there, only breath.  
> Drawn by the graceful birds, wishing to hear  
> Their gentle speech, I seated myself upon a platform.  
> After a little while, a long-tailed bird  
> Raised the question, “Who is he?”  
> A long-beaked crow, well acquainted with people,  
> Looked at me for a long time and said:  
> “He is no bow-bearing hunter who hunts us all;  
> No stony-hearted villain; no sinner  
> Who has come to trap and carry us away.”  
> “Then who is he?” asked a mynah.  
> A house pigeon opened its beak: “This fellow  
> Is a Tamilian swarming with foolish beliefs!  
> Though claiming his race possesses sixfold reason,  
> He belongs to the doomed crowd that fights like dogs!”  
> They laughed without restraint, asking in turn,  
> “Him? Is that really him?” and humiliated me.  
> Then a little sparrow said: “Among us there is no division;  
> We do not enslave our females. But he belongs  
> To the petty, evil-living crowd that argues for many divisions  
> Among people, enslaves women, and speaks of them with contempt.”  
> The others burst into a deep chorus of “Ah! Ah! Ah!”  
> Then the anril bird came forward and said:  
> “In this worldly life we enjoy happiness, reject sorrow,  
> And rejoice always in the sweet love of our pure females.  
> But humans reject it, invent an unseen world,  
> Weep while clinging to scripture, uproot the crop of love,  
> Call it fate, and, unable to find the path of joy,  
> Forever seek suffering and perish in grief!  
> He is one descended from that human multitude  
> Which claims sixfold knowledge. By what reason, then,  
> Can we call him superior to us?”  
> “Shame! Do not speak of his condition. Humans without honour  
> Will still declare themselves superior,” said the noble swan.  
> All cried “Shame!” till the heavens shook;  
> Ashamed, I bowed my head. Then a woodpecker continued:  
> “Through our own effort we live upon the earth.  
> He belongs to the lazy crowd that wails  
> That without an act of God it cannot save itself in the least.”  
> “Yes, yes! Their moving and standing, eating and sleeping,  
> Cheating the town, wishing ruin on their neighbours,  
> Becoming masters of the property of the deceived,  
> And committing ever more varieties of evil:  
> All of it is His doing!” a parrot said in mockery.  
> All the birds around laughed till the grove resounded  
> And cried aloud in scorn, “This fellow?”  
> All these words struck my heart, altered my thoughts,  
> And kindled my anger. “Shall these wretched birds mock me?  
> Shall I depart in silence without curbing their tongues?”  
> Thinking thus, I came forward, seeking a reply to give;  
> But I could not do it!  
> Humiliated by birds of five senses, my strength failed.  
> I threw a stone at the birds that had come to declare  
> That corruption saturated the lives of rational humans.  
> At that moment the splendid peacock said:  
> “They possess mature reason, indeed, yet they have achieved  
> Nothing through discerning knowledge. They praise the ancient state  
> While their present greatness sinks into disgrace. Moreover,  
> Unlike us, without freedom or rightful liberty,  
> He belongs to the slaves who let foreigners rule their country.”  
> “Alas, how cruel his condition, terribly cruel!  
> Having lost his country and gained no welfare,  
> Do not mock the man who has fallen into ruin,”  
> Said the royal eagle. The others, their eyes wet, cried, “Pity!”  
> Having first reviled me greatly, the birds then pitied me  
> And grieved deeply. Shorn of my pride by their speech,  
> I sat in the little upper room, unable to draw a line of verse:  
> “Why did I seek this grove?” I lamented in anguish.  
> Looking at me, a sparrow said,  
> “Until every foolish act has wholly ceased,  
> Do not seek self-rule,” and flew away.  
> Hearing it, I rose in haste. “Brave sons of pure Tamil land,  
> Let us strive so that the mocking words with which  
> These birds scorned us may be proved false!” I declared.  
> But the birds, laughing, said, “You cannot do it  
> Until every act of folly has wholly ceased;  
> Do not seek self-rule,” and flew away.

Revolutionary poet Bharathidasan deserves our praise not merely because he employs his talent for an artistic revolution, but because he has created a revolutionary attitude toward art in Tamil Nadu. That is his victory!

Formerly, creatures from the snake to the sparrow were despised and ridiculed by humankind. Now they are made professors who ridicule man’s condition, thoughts, and deeds and teach him wisdom. This is indeed a new method, is it not? It is an artistic revolution!

17-11-1946

---NOTES---
The Tamil source is retained verbatim in the bilingual output. The title and closing date were checked against the first and final scans. Several portions of the OCR, especially the long poem by Puduvai Sivaprakasam, contain damaged characters, dropped words, and broken lineation. The English rendering follows the legible scan and context conservatively; uncertain repetitions and damaged transitions have not been presented as exact quotations from a separately verified modern edition. The OCR string printed beside “mole” on the second scan is corrupt, but the accompanying animal names are translated from the legible Tamil context. “Five-lettered and six-lettered formulas” refers to religious mantras named by their number of syllables or letters. “Anril” is retained as the conventional Tamil literary bird name.
